#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tools/mutate — the mutation round: break a game one line at a time, demand the run notices.

A round injects one-line faults into a game under `games/` and asks, per fault,
whether `cargo test -p <game>` or the game's `--verify` fails. A fault neither
notices is an escape — a check that is looser than it reads. The technique and
its two traps are `docs/api/jidousha-testing.md` ("Mutate the game and check the
run notices"); this is the instrument for adopted games (ADR-0047). E0 authors
still write their own, and that is part of what E0 measures.

Usage:  tools/mutate <game> <list>... [--jobs N] [--fast] [--changed-since REV]
                                      [--only ID ...] [--fresh]

A list is `games/<game>/mutants/*.txt` (a path, or one relative to the game's
directory; a glob is expanded either way). One fault per block of four lines:

    ### label — what the fault is
    @ src/witness.rs
    - the exact text to find (it must occur exactly once in the file)
    + the text that replaces it

`⏎` in either text stands for a line break; a line starting `#!` is a header.
Several lists are one round, each label prefixed by its list's stem (`w9:C1`).

Per fault: build `-p <package>` (tests, then the binary), run `cargo test`, then
the game's verify exactly as `tools/test` runs it (`tools/verify`'s command and
judgement, loaded from that file). Noticed if either fails. The two hard errors:
a find that matches other than once stops the round before anything is written,
and a fault that does not build is NOT BUILT — never noticed. Files are restored
from the bytes read; every worktree is checked clean after the round; a check
that does not end in ten minutes is noticed, and its process group is killed.

Workers run in persistent worktrees, `target/mutants/w<n>`: detached, moved to
HEAD and checked clean at round start, and built unmutated before the first
fault, so their `target/` stays warm across rounds. `--fresh` recreates them.
`--fast` skips verify for a fault the tests already noticed (`verify=-`; the
"verify alone" count is withheld). `--changed-since REV` keeps a fault iff its
`@` file differs between REV and HEAD.

Exit codes: 0 the round ran and is scored (escapes are findings, not faults of
the tool) · 2 the round could not be scored (a hard error; nothing was counted)
· 130 interrupted (every file written back; nothing scored).

Key functions: `parse_list`, `resolve_lists`, `check_matches`, `filter_changed`,
`check_fault`, `judge`, `mutate_once`, `prepare_worktree`, `summarize`, `main`.
Depends on: the Python 3.8+ standard library, `git`, `cargo`, and `tools/verify`.
INVARIANT: a fault is noticed only if it built; NOT BUILT is its own verdict.
INVARIANT: every file a fault writes is written back from the bytes read before it.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import glob
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import queue
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
from dataclasses import asdict, dataclass
from datetime import datetime, timezone
